add(H2("Abstract"));
add(P_(`India publishes its electoral rolls as image-only PDFs: public in law, unreadable by machine, and beyond independent audit. In the 2025–26 Special Intensive Revision (SIR) the Uttar Pradesh roll fell from 15.44 crore electors to a 12.56 crore draft and ended at 13.40 crore, figures the public could not check. We describe an open pipeline that reads scanned Hindi rolls on a CPU-only server with no paid model: field-specific Tesseract passes, consensus on Devanagari names within and across documents, local transliteration, a pixel-level detector for cards printed struck through, and an uncertainty flag on every card. Applied to the complete SIR final roll of Gautam Buddh Nagar (Noida, Dadri, Jewar), ${n(D.parts)} parts and ${n(C.pages)} pages, it read ${n(D.rows)} cards, ${pct(sum((a) => a.rows), sum((a) => a.maxSerialSum))} of the printed serial numbers. On a 300-card test set from ten random pages it read ${B3.fully} cards fully correct (95% interval ${wilson(B3.fully, 300)}), every age and ${B3.fields.gender} of 300 genders; most remaining errors are two diagnosed systematic misreads in Noida's EPIC series and house numbers. The final roll shows ${n(D.added)} cards (${pct(D.added, D.rows)}) added after the draft, young and spread over ${pct(sum((a) => a.sectionsWithAdd), sum((a) => a.sections), 0)} of localities, at least ${pct(MOVES + (X ? X.deletedAndAdded : 0), D.added, 0)} of them re-enrolments after a move; ${n(D.deleted ?? 0)} cards printed as deleted after the draft; and EPIC numbers that appear twice which are moves, within and between constituencies, rather than duplicate enrolment. The far larger pre-draft deletions can be measured only against the roll the revision replaced. Image-only publication neither protects electors nor informs the public; we set out the aggregates the Commission could release instead.`));
