add(H2("6.8 External consistency"));
add(P_("Quantities derived from the Commission's published figures can be compared with what the pipeline read. The press figures for the district, a 23.98% (4.47 lakh) reduction at the draft and a 19.33% net reduction at the final roll [2, 3], imply a pre-revision roll of about " + lakh(gbnPreSir) + ", a draft of " + lakh(gbnDraft) + " and a final roll of " + lakh(gbnFinalPress) + ". The cards we read that are not marked as additions, " + n(D.rows - D.added) + ", are within " + pct(Math.abs(D.rows - D.added - gbnDraft), gbnDraft, 1) + " of the implied draft. The implied net gain between draft and final roll is " + n(netGainPress) + " electors; we count " + n(D.added) + " additions and " + n(D.deleted ?? stampedTotal) + " cards printed as deleted, a net gain of " + n(D.added - (D.deleted ?? stampedTotal)) + ", " + n(Math.abs(D.added - (D.deleted ?? stampedTotal) - netGainPress)) + " from the implied figure on a base of 1.5 million. The sex ratio as read is " + D.sexRatio + " women per 1,000 men (" + ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].sexRatio + " in " + A[ac].name).join(", ") + ") against " + P.stateSexRatio + " state-wide, and the share aged 18–19 is " + pct(D.age18_19, D.electors ?? D.rows, 2) + " against 1.32%; a migrant-receiving district need not match the state, so these are checks of magnitude only. The district administration's lists of electors with no mapping or a logical discrepancy [15] would be a sharper check and have not yet been used."));
add(H2("6.9 Error analysis"));
add(P_("The error classes, in order of consequence, are these. A card printed as deleted and read as a live elector, which defeated the text passes without tripping any check until the detector was added (Section 8.4). A plausible-but-wrong EPIC: the first letter of the series dropped and a following 0 read as O, so that ZYH0373001 becomes YHO0373001, which fits the format and on which three reads agree because they share the crop; neither the format check nor the vote bounds it, and what does is the series, since a prefix the Commission has not issued is a misread (" + pct(PFX[61].YHO + PFX[61].YHS, PFX[61].total) + " of Noida's numbers, " + pct(PFX[62].NOT, PFX[62].total) + " of Dadri's, digits intact on the benchmark evidence). A repair that maps a rare prefix to the issued series one letter away would correct most without a re-read (paper/analysis/epic_series_repair.cjs measures its scope); it has not been applied here. Then look-alike Devanagari glyphs in names, mostly repaired by consensus when a trusted token exists; Latin letters and punctuation lost in house numbers; and cards whose grid cell was mislocated, which produce a blank field and a flag."));

// =====================================================================
// 7 AUDIT FRAMEWORK
// =====================================================================
add(H1("7. Methodology II: an audit framework for roll revisions (RQ3)"));
add(H2("7.1 Audit questions"));
add(P_("Given the published documents of a revision, each elector row carries an Action_status (existing, added, deleted, modified) and the list it came from. The audit asks, for each booth and for the constituency: how many electors were added, deleted and modified; whether the changes are concentrated in particular booths or sections; whether the age and gender composition of the changed electors differs from that of the roll; whether the same elector appears more than once; and whether the roll's structure (age, sex ratio, household size) is what the demography of the place leads one to expect."));
add(H2("7.2 Units of analysis"));
add(P_("Constituency, part (booth), section (locality) and house number. A house number is a group of electors of one section who share a printed house number after print noise (“H.No 195”, “Hno-195”, “195”) is folded; in an urban roll it is usually a flat, in a rural roll often a compound. Cards printed with the DELETED stamp (Section 8.4) are post-draft deletions and are excluded from the structural figures, as the Commission's own counts exclude them; the pipeline detects them in Noida and Dadri and a separate scan in Jewar (Section 8.4)."));
add(H2("7.3 Indicators"));
add(table(["Indicator", "Definition", "Needs", "Computed here"],
  [["Addition rate", "added / final electors", "final roll", "yes, by part and section"],
   ["Modification rate", "modified / final electors", "final roll", "yes, by part"],
   ["Deletion rate", "deleted / (existing + deleted)", "final roll for post-draft deletions (stamped cards); draft roll for the rest", "post-draft only"],
   ["Age and sex profile of additions", "share of additions in each age band and sex against the roll", "final roll", "yes"],
   ["Duplicate registration", "EPIC numbers that appear in more than one part", "final roll", "yes"],
   ["Sex ratio", "women per 1,000 men, by part", "final roll", "yes"],
   ["House-number sharing", "distribution of electors per house number", "final roll", "yes"],
   ["Whole-household deletion", "house numbers of two or more electors all of whom were deleted", "draft and final roll", "no"],
   ["Booth outlier score", "robust z-score of a booth's rate within the constituency (median and MAD)", "any rate", "yes, for additions"]],
  [1.5, 2.6, 1.6, 1.3], "Table 6. Audit indicators, what each needs, and which are computed in Section 8."));
add(H2("7.4 Statistical treatment"));
add(P_("Booth-level rates are reported as medians with the 10th and 90th percentiles across booths, and outliers are identified with a robust z-score (median and median absolute deviation), because the distributions are skewed and a few small booths would dominate a mean. Proportions are reported with Wilson 95% intervals where the denominator is small. With denominators in the tens of thousands, as for the age and sex composition of additions against the roll, the intervals are a fraction of a percentage point and the differences of Section 8.2 are many times wider, so a chi-squared test on banded counts rejects equality trivially and its statistic is not reported. Where booths are geocoded, spatial autocorrelation of booth rates can be measured with Moran's I; we did not do so."));
add(H2("7.5 Carrying extraction error into the audit"));
add(P_("Every rate in Section 8.1 is a ratio of card counts whose numerator comes from a list header and whose denominator is a count of cards. Neither depends on reading a name, an EPIC or an age, so two extraction errors reach them: a page whose header could not be read is assumed to be main roll (21 of " + n(J.pages) + " pages in Jewar), and a part read short loses cards. The loss is not even: the cards the layout engine misses lie at the ends of lists, and the additions list is short and last, so a part that is 2% short may have lost a larger share of its additions. A single part's addition rate is read with that in mind; at constituency level a shortfall under 1% cannot move a 5–7% addition rate by more than a few tenths of a point. Indicators that depend on a field are computed on the rows where it was readable."));

// =====================================================================
// 8 RESULTS
// =====================================================================
add(H1("8. Results: Gautam Buddh Nagar after the revision"));
add(H2("8.1 Scale of change in the final roll"));
add(table(["", name("61"), name("62"), name("63"), "District"],
  [["Cards in the final roll (rows read)", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].rows)), n(D.rows)],
   ["Printed as deleted after the draft", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(DEL(ac)) + " (" + pct(DEL(ac), A[ac].rows, 2) + ")"), n(D.deleted ?? 0) + " (" + pct(D.deleted ?? 0, D.rows, 2) + ")"],
   ["Electors", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(E(ac))), n(D.electors ?? D.rows)],
   ["Marked “existing” (carried from the draft)", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].action.existing)), n(sum((a) => a.action.existing))],
   ["Marked “added”", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].action.added) + " (" + pct(A[ac].action.added, A[ac].rows) + ")"), n(D.added) + " (" + pct(D.added, D.rows) + ")"],
   ["Marked “modified”", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].action.modified) + " (" + pct(A[ac].action.modified, A[ac].rows, 2) + ")"), n(D.modified) + " (" + pct(D.modified, D.rows, 2) + ")"],
   ["Sections with at least one addition", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].sectionsWithAdd) + " of " + n(A[ac].sections)), n(sum((a) => a.sectionsWithAdd)) + " of " + n(sum((a) => a.sections))],
   ["Addition rate by part: median (10th–90th pct.)", ...ACS.map((ac) => (100 * A[ac].addRate.median).toFixed(1) + "% (" + (100 * A[ac].addRate.p10).toFixed(1) + "–" + (100 * A[ac].addRate.p90).toFixed(1) + "%)"), ""],
   ["Women among additions", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].addedGender.F) + " of " + n(A[ac].addedGender.F + A[ac].addedGender.M) + " (" + pct(A[ac].addedGender.F, A[ac].addedGender.F + A[ac].addedGender.M) + ")"), pct(sum((a) => a.addedGender.F), sum((a) => a.addedGender.F + a.addedGender.M))],
   ["Additions aged 18–29", ...ACS.map((ac) => pct(A[ac].added18_29, A[ac].action.added)), pct(sum((a) => a.added18_29), D.added)],
   ["Median age of additions / of the roll", ...ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].addedMedianAge + " / " + A[ac].medianAge.all), ""]],
  [2.3, 1.3, 1.3, 1.3, 1.3], "Table 7. What the final roll records about the revision. Women among additions are counted over rows with a readable gender."));
add(P_("Between a twentieth and a thirteenth of the final roll is new: " + n(D.added) + " electors, " + pct(D.added, D.rows) + " of the district, were added between the draft of 6 January and the final roll of 10 April 2026, and " + n(D.modified) + " entries (" + pct(D.modified, D.rows, 2) + ") were modified. Dadri added the most, in number and in share; Noida the least. The additions are not concentrated: " + pct(sum((a) => a.sectionsWithAdd), sum((a) => a.sections), 0) + " of the " + n(sum((a) => a.sections)) + " sections in the district gained at least one elector, and in every constituency the middle 80% of booths gained between about 1% and 14% of their electors (Table 7)."));
add(H2("8.2 Who was added"));
add(figure("fig9_age_pyramids.png", "Figure 10. Age–sex structure of the district's final roll (left) and of the electors marked as added in it (right), as read by the pipeline; rows without a readable age and gender are excluded.", 6.6));
add(P_("The roll as a whole has the shape of a working-age city (median age " + A["61"].medianAge.all + " in Noida, " + A["63"].medianAge.all + " in Jewar); the additions are young (Figure 10): their median age is " + A["61"].addedMedianAge + " in Noida, " + A["62"].addedMedianAge + " in Dadri and " + A["63"].addedMedianAge + " in Jewar, and " + pct(A["63"].added18_29, A["63"].action.added, 0) + " of Jewar's additions are aged 18–29 against " + pct(A["61"].added18_29, A["61"].action.added, 0) + " of Noida's. The rural constituency's additions are overwhelmingly first-time electors; the urban one's are spread across working ages. Women are " + pct(A["61"].addedGender.F, A["61"].addedGender.F + A["61"].addedGender.M) + " of Noida's additions and " + pct(A["62"].addedGender.F, A["62"].addedGender.F + A["62"].addedGender.M) + " of Dadri's, against " + pct(A["61"].gender.F, A["61"].gender.F + A["61"].gender.M) + " and " + pct(A["62"].gender.F, A["62"].gender.F + A["62"].gender.M) + " of the roll. Not every addition is a new elector: at least " + n(MOVES) + " (" + pct(MOVES, D.added) + ") are re-enrolments of an elector whose old card is printed as deleted elsewhere in the constituency, and " + (X ? n(X.deletedAndAdded) : "some") + " more are moves between constituencies (Section 8.4). For the women's share, marriage migration and under-enrolment in the draft are both possible; the final roll cannot tell them apart."));
add(H2("8.3 Variation across booths"));
add(figure("fig11_booth_distributions.png", "Figure 11. Booth-level distributions. Left: electors added as a share of the part. Right: women per 1,000 men in the part, for parts with more than 50 men.", 7.0));
add(P_("Addition rates have a long right tail in every constituency (Figure 11). The extremes are part 163 of Jewar, where 61% of electors are new, part 786 of Dadri, a booth of 54 electors of whom 27 are new, and three Noida parts with 23–24%. A high rate is not in itself a fault: a new housing society or a booth split from an old one looks exactly like this. What the audit provides is the list of booths that merit the question. Sex ratios tell the three places apart: in Jewar nearly every part lies between 650 and 1,000 women per 1,000 men (booth median " + boothMedianSR("63") + "); in Noida they run from below 500 to above 1,200 (median " + boothMedianSR("61") + "), the highest at the clubhouses of gated housing societies and the lowest in urban villages where men who have come for work outnumber women. A booth's sex ratio describes who lives there; only a change from its own earlier roll would mark it for scrutiny."));
add(H2("8.4 Electors who appear twice, and the cards printed as deleted"));
add(table(["", name("61"), name("62"), name("63")],
  [["EPIC numbers found in two parts of the constituency", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].dupEpics))],
   ["… as a share of cards", ...ACS.map((ac) => pct(A[ac].dupEpics, A[ac].rows, 2))],
   ["… one of the cards printed as deleted", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].dupOverlap.withDeleted ?? 0) + " (" + pct(A[ac].dupOverlap.withDeleted ?? 0, A[ac].dupOverlap.groups, 0) + ")")],
   ["… both cards live, same name and age", ...ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].dupOverlap.liveOnlySameName ?? 0))],
   ["… both cards live, different names", ...ACS.map((ac) => X ? n(X.withinAcDiffNameLive[ac].groups) : "—")],
   ["Cards printed as deleted: pipeline (final run)", ...ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].deletedSource === "pipeline" ? n(DEL(ac)) + " (" + pct(DEL(ac), A[ac].rows, 2) + ")" : "run predates the detector")],
   ["Cards printed as deleted: independent pixel scan of every card page", ...ACS.map((ac) => spv(ac, (s) => n(s.stamped) + " (" + pct(s.stamped, s.cards, 2) + ")"))],
   ["Deleted cards whose EPIC is added again in the constituency, same age", ...ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].deletedStats ? n(A[ac].deletedStats.readdedSameAge) + " (" + pct(A[ac].deletedStats.readdedSameAge, A[ac].deletedStats.count, 0) + ")" : "—")],
   ["Parts with no deleted card / highest share in one part", ...ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].deletedStats ? A[ac].deletedStats.rate.partsWithNone + " of " + A[ac].parts + " / " + pct(A[ac].deletedStats.rate.max, 1, 0) : "—")]],
  [2.8, 1.2, 1.2, 1.2], "Table 8. EPIC numbers that occur in two parts of the same constituency, and cards printed with the DELETED stamp. Jewar's deleted cards are those of the pixel scan that could be joined to a row by page position (" + n(DEL("63")) + "); duplicate groups are counted over all cards."));
add(P_("An EPIC number is meant to identify one elector, yet " + ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].dupEpics) + " numbers occur in two parts of " + A[ac].name).join(", ") + " (Table 8). They are not double enrolments. In " + pct(A["61"].dupOverlap.withDeleted ?? 0, A["61"].dupOverlap.groups, 0) + " of Noida's pairs and " + pct(A["62"].dupOverlap.withDeleted ?? 0, A["62"].dupOverlap.groups, 0) + " of Dadri's, one card carries a grey DELETED watermark printed diagonally across it, with a reason letter in its serial box (S shifted, E expired, R duplicate), and the other is in the additions list of another part: the elector moved, was enrolled afresh, and the old card was printed struck through rather than removed. The text passes do not see the stamp. They read such a card as a live elector with a garbled name and no gender, and an early reading of the district carried every one of them as an existing elector while passing every format check; the pattern was found only by cutting 24 implausible pairs out of the PDFs and looking at them. The detector of Section 5.5 finds the stamp from the pixels. It ran inside the pipeline for Noida and Dadri, and as an independent scan of every card page for all three rolls; the two counts agree to within 3%."));
add(P_("With the deleted cards set aside almost nothing is left of the duplicates. Only " + ACS.map((ac) => n(A[ac].dupOverlap.liveOnlySameName ?? 0)).join(", ") + " pairs in the three constituencies are two live cards with the same name and age, the signature of an elector enrolled twice; the pairs of live cards with different names (" + (X ? ACS.map((ac) => X.withinAcDiffNameLive[ac].groups).join(", ") : "—") + ") are mostly EPIC misreads that collide (Section 6.3) and a few reused numbers. Seen from the other side, most deleted cards have no twin: only " + ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].deletedStats ? pct(A[ac].deletedStats.readdedSameAge, A[ac].deletedStats.count, 0) : "—").join(", ") + " of them have an EPIC that is added again in the same constituency. The rest are electors removed after the draft as dead, shifted away or enrolled twice, and they are unevenly spread: many parts have none, and the highest has " + pct(Math.max(...ACS.map((ac) => A[ac].deletedStats ? A[ac].deletedStats.rate.max : 0)), 1, 0) + " of its cards struck through, a concentration we have not explained."));

add(P_("The same holds across constituencies (Table 8b). " + (X ? n(X.groups) + " EPIC numbers occur in two of the three constituencies and none in all three; " + n(X.pairs["61-62"] || 0) + " are between Noida and Dadri, which share a boundary and a housing market. " + n(X.deletedAndAdded) + " of them (" + pct(X.deletedAndAdded, X.groups, 0) + ") are a card deleted in one constituency and a card added in the other, a move across the line. " + n(X.bothLive) + " numbers are live in both, and " + n(X.bothLiveSameNameSameAge) + " of those carry the same name and age on both cards, " + pct(X.bothLiveSameNameSameAge, D.electors ?? D.rows, 4) + " of the district's electors." : "") + " The district shows no measurable multiple enrolment by EPIC number, within or between its constituencies. The limit of that statement is the key: an elector enrolled under two different numbers is invisible to it, and the prefix misreads of Section 6.3 can hide a pair but cannot create one."));
if (X) add(table(["", "Count"],
  [["EPIC numbers that occur in two constituencies", n(X.groups)], ["… Noida–Dadri / Dadri–Jewar / Noida–Jewar", `${n(X.pairs["61-62"] || 0)} / ${n(X.pairs["62-63"] || 0)} / ${n(X.pairs["61-63"] || 0)}`], ["… in all three", n(X.inAllThree)],
   ["… one card deleted, the other added (a move across the line)", `${n(X.deletedAndAdded)} (${pct(X.deletedAndAdded, X.groups, 0)})`], ["… same name on both cards", n(X.sameName)], ["… different names, same age (name read through the stamp)", n(X.diffNameSameAge)],
   ["… live in both constituencies", n(X.bothLive)], ["… live in both, same name and age", n(X.bothLiveSameNameSameAge)],
   ["Within a constituency: two live cards, same EPIC, different names (Noida / Dadri / Jewar)", `${n(X.withinAcDiffNameLive[61].groups)} / ${n(X.withinAcDiffNameLive[62].groups)} / ${n(X.withinAcDiffNameLive[63].groups)}`]],
  [4.6, 1.8], "Table 8b. EPIC numbers shared across the three constituencies of the district. Names are compared as read."));
add(H2("8.5 The structure of the roll"));
add(figure("fig10_age_heaping.png", "Figure 12. Electors by single year of age, three constituencies together. The heaps lie at ages ending in 1 and the troughs at ages ending in 0: round ages reported at an earlier enumeration, advanced by one year to the revision's qualifying date.", 7.0));
add(P_("Self-reported ages in India heap on multiples of five and ten. In this roll the heaps sit one year above the round numbers (Figure 12): Jewar has " + n(A["63"].ageHist["60"]) + " electors aged 60 and " + n(A["63"].ageHist["61"]) + " aged 61, " + n(A["63"].ageHist["70"]) + " aged 70 and " + n(A["63"].ageHist["71"]) + " aged 71. Our hypothesis, not verified against an earlier roll, is that round ages reported at an earlier enumeration were advanced by a year to the qualifying date; a date-of-birth field completed with a default would produce the same step. The pattern is sharpest in rural Jewar and faint in Noida. Its survival is evidence that the age field is read faithfully, and a caution for anyone banding ages at round boundaries."));
add(figure("fig12_household_size.png", "Figure 13. Electors sharing one printed house number within a section, as a share of all house numbers in the constituency.", 5.2));
add(P_("House numbers show the urban–rural gradient at its sharpest (Figure 13). In Noida " + pct(A["61"].hhSize["1"], A["61"].households, 0) + " of house numbers carry a single elector and " + pct(A["61"].hhSize["10+"], A["61"].households) + " carry ten or more; in Jewar " + pct(A["63"].hhSize["1"], A["63"].households, 0) + " carry one and " + pct(A["63"].hhSize["10+"], A["63"].households) + " ten or more. A Noida number is a flat; a Jewar number is often a compound or a hamlet, so any household-level indicator, including whole-household deletion, means different things in the two. Women listed under a father rather than a husband are " + pct(A["63"].relByGender["F|Father"], A["63"].relByGender["F|Father"] + A["63"].relByGender["F|Husband"]) + " in Jewar and " + pct(A["61"].relByGender["F|Father"], A["61"].relByGender["F|Father"] + A["61"].relByGender["F|Husband"]) + " in Noida, a difference that is partly age structure and should be compared within age bands before it is read as custom."));
add(P_("One question the roll can answer directly is whether it carries anyone too young to be on it. So far as the printed age can tell, it does not: after the re-read, " + pl(sum((a) => a.ageLt18.a10_17), "elector") + " in the district " + (sum((a) => a.ageLt18.a10_17) === 1 ? "is" : "are") + " printed with an age between 10 and 17 (Section 6.7), which is within the misread rate of the age field."));
add(H2("8.6 What the final roll shows of deletions, and what it cannot"));
add(P_("The revision's most contested quantity is the number of electors removed, and the final roll shows part of it. An elector removed after the draft stays on the page with the DELETED stamp, so the stamped cards count post-draft deletions by booth: " + ACS.map((ac) => n(DEL(ac)) + " (" + pct(DEL(ac), A[ac].rows, 2) + ") in " + A[ac].name).join(", ") + ", " + n(D.deleted ?? stampedTotal) + " in the district. The deletions made before the draft are the ones the debate turned on, and those electors are simply not printed. Our count of " + n(D.electors ?? electors) + " electors is " + (100 * (1 - (D.electors ?? electors) / gbnPreSir)).toFixed(1) + "% below the pre-revision roll the press figures imply, against the 19.33% reported [2, 3]; that is a consistency check, not a measurement. Measuring pre-draft deletions requires the roll the revision replaced, published in the same image-only form, which we did not collect; with it the pipeline would deliver every deletion indicator of Table 6 at booth level, including the age and sex of the deleted and the house numbers emptied entirely. The district administration's lists of electors with no mapping [15] are a partial record of that stage. That is the natural next run."));
add(H2("8.7 Robustness"));
add(P_("The addition and modification counts of Table 7 come from list headers and card counts and are unaffected by field-level error; a part's addition rate can be off by as much as the part is short, which is under 2% for the great majority. The age and sex figures exclude rows without a readable age or gender (" + ACS.map((ac) => pct(unreadGender(A[ac]), E(ac), 2) + " in " + A[ac].name).join(", ") + "). The duplicate counts are computed on EPICs in the current format and are, if anything, undercounts, since a misread EPIC cannot match its twin."));

// =====================================================================
// 9 DISCUSSION
// =====================================================================
add(H1("9. Discussion"));
add(H2("9.1 What the evaluation shows"));
add(P_("On a roll for which a template and a hand-checked page exist, the local engine read " + B3.fully + " of 300 test cards fully correct, every age and " + B3.fields.gender + " of 300 genders, names with one error in twenty, and flagged " + B3.wrongFlagged + " of its " + (300 - B3.fully) + " wrong cards; it also misread the series prefix of one Noida EPIC in eight, a systematic fault that the test set found and the development page could not, read a complete district to within 1% of the printed serial numbers, and reproduced to within a few thousand the net change in electors that the Commission's published figures imply. That is sufficient for booth-level aggregate audit, where a one-in-twenty name error does not change an addition rate, provided the bounds of Section 7.5 are carried through. It is not sufficient for individual-level claims about a named elector, and we make none."));
add(H2("9.2 Implications for election administration"));
add(P_("The principal implication is that image-only publication does not prevent structured extraction; it raises the cost of extraction to a level that favours well-resourced actors over citizens and researchers. A rented server reads a constituency overnight. If the Commission's concern is bulk harvesting of personal data, rasterisation does not address it; if its concern is public scrutiny of revisions, rasterisation serves it only through obscurity. The second implication is specific to the revision: the final roll alone, however completely read, cannot answer the question that mattered most in public debate. The documents that could, the draft and the deletion lists, are published in the same form."));
add(H2("9.3 Implications for low-resource document AI"));
add(P_("Four design choices generalise beyond electoral rolls, and the evaluation of this paper adds three lessons about how to measure them. Field-specific recognition passes (crop, whitelist, scale, script model chosen per field) beat a single generic pass by a wide margin on structured forms: on the development page they take EPIC from 23 to 30 of 30 before any consensus runs, and on the test set they read every age and 296 of 300 genders. Single-edit consensus against trusted tokens repairs the dominant Devanagari error classes without a language model, and the restriction to one edit of a known kind is what keeps it from inventing names. A flag taxonomy with a tunable threshold, combined with consistency checks that the whole corpus must pass, turns an OCR system into an instrument whose uncertainty is reported and whose improvement between builds can be measured without new ground truth, which is what Table 5b did. And the pixel-level stamp detector is an instance of a general point: a document's semantics are not all in its text, and a reader that only reads text will carry an overprinted deletion as a live record while passing every text-level check."));
add(P_("The measurement lessons are these. First, a development page measures the pipeline on what it was built to read; the test set of Section 6.3, drawn after the pipeline was frozen, found two systematic faults in a constituency the development page did not cover, and a reader of any document-AI result should ask which of the two kinds of page it was scored on. Second, errors in structured extraction are systematic, not random: they attach to a print style, a font, a series of numbers, a crop, and a single accuracy figure for a corpus understates them where they cluster and overstates them elsewhere. The split by constituency in Table 2c is cheap and should be standard. Third, a synthetic page in the document's geometry, with invented content, is a regression test that costs nothing in personal data and reproduces the faults that matter (Appendix B); it cannot replace real pages, whose print and scan artefacts it lacks, but it catches a crop or whitelist regression before a re-read of a million cards does. Taken together with the closed-vocabulary and series checks, these give a low-resource project a way to know its error rate on a corpus it cannot afford to annotate, which is the usual condition of such projects: thirty cards cost an hour, three hundred a day, and three million are read overnight."));
add(P_("What does not generalise should be said as plainly. The pipeline reads one kind of document: machine-printed, fixed-template, laser-quality, in a script Tesseract has a model for. It has nothing to offer on handwriting, on degraded scans of older rolls, on templates it has not been given, or on scripts whose models are weak; GlotOCR Bench's finding that coverage falls off a cliff beyond a few dozen scripts [30] applies to the engine underneath it as much as to any vision model. For those cases the vision-language route of Section 3.2 is the right one, and the contribution of this paper to it is the flag: a local engine that knows when it is unsure can send a model only the cards that need it, at a cost that scales with the error rate rather than the page count."));
add(H2("9.4 Is image-only publication a privacy safeguard?"));
add(P_("On the evidence of this paper it is not an effective one. A rented server read a district, and anyone with the same budget can do the same, so the format does not stop extraction; it decides who can afford it, and that is the well-resourced rather than the public. At the same time it makes the privacy-preserving alternative, official publication of booth-level aggregate statistics on additions and deletions by age band and sex, with no names, look unnecessary. We cannot measure how much harvesting the format deters. We can say that every number in Section 8 could be published by the Commission from data it already holds, at no risk to any elector, and would then not need to be reconstructed from images."));
add(H2("9.5 Recommendations"));
add(bullet("For the Commission: publish, with each draft and final roll, a booth-level table of elector counts and of additions, deletions and modifications by age band and sex, and a machine-readable part list, which it already serves to its own portal."));
add(bullet("For researchers and civil society: use validated, open extraction with reported error rates and consistency checks; publish aggregates only; fix the audit indicators before the documents are read."));
add(bullet("For tool builders: ship uncertainty flags and a benchmark page for every print template, and treat a rise in the consistency-check failure rate as a signal to stop and fix the template rather than noise to filter."));
add(H2("9.6 Generalisability"));
add(P_("The layout engine is template-driven and the Uttar Pradesh template was used unchanged on all three constituencies; the same template has since been applied to other Hindi-belt districts, and a Madhya Pradesh roll was read with the default template with only the section header unrecognised. Adding a state means adding a template and a benchmark page. The transliteration engine is Hindi-specific; Marathi, Telugu and Kannada rolls need their own rules and seed corpora."));

add(H2("9.7 Threats to validity and open questions"));
add(P_("A reader should weigh the following before relying on any number in Section 8. We list them because each changes how far a result can be pushed, and because several of them can be settled by work we name."));
add(bullet([b("The builder evaluated the system. "), run("One author wrote the pipeline, chose and transcribed the development page and tuned the thresholds on it, so the test set of Section 6.3, drawn after the pipeline was frozen, is the figure to quote: 81% of cards, not 90%. That set was transcribed and adjudicated by a vision-language model, the kind of reader Section 3.2 warns is fluent when wrong; Surya, which shares nothing with either reader, is a partial third opinion, and every accuracy figure is reproduced by a shipped command. The test that would settle it is a human transcription of the same ten pages.")]));
add(bullet([b("What “added” measures. "), run("A row is “added” because it was printed in the additions list of the final roll. That list holds genuinely new electors and also re-enrolments after a move within the constituency, whose old card is the stamped one. The stamped cards with a twin among the additions (Table 8) are therefore the floor of the share of additions that are moves rather than new electors" + (allScanned ? ": " + ACS.map((ac) => pct(SP(ac).readdedSameAge, A[ac].action.added) + " in " + A[ac].name).join(", ") : "") + ". The addition rates of Table 7 are rates of cards printed as added, and a reader who wants new electors should subtract that share.")]));
add(bullet([b("Press-derived baselines. "), run("The 23.98% and 19.33% figures for the district come from press reports of Commission data, rounded, with denominators we infer rather than read. Agreement with them to a fraction of a percent (Section 6.8) is a plausibility check and could be partly coincidental; it is not a validation of any field, and we have not found a Commission document that states the district's totals directly.")]));
add(bullet([b("Errors are not random. "), run("The pipeline's failures cluster: the overprinted deleted cards produced blank genders and garbled names, so the unreadable rows excluded from the age and sex figures are disproportionately deleted electors, which is the right exclusion, but older, poorly printed or rural cards may also be over-represented among them in ways we have not measured. A random-error model understates this; the consistency checks bound the rate of failure, not its distribution.")]));
add(bullet([b("Systematic error is not random error. "), run("The EPIC prefix and house-number faults of Section 6.3 are concentrated in one constituency and one print style, so a field accuracy quoted for the district understates the error where it matters and overstates it elsewhere; Table 2c gives the split, and any user of the released aggregates should read Noida's EPIC-based figures with that in mind.")]));
add(bullet([b("Jewar's deleted cards come from a join. "), run("For Noida and Dadri the final run writes the deleted status itself. Jewar's run predates the detector, so its stamped cards are tied to rows by part, page and reading order, and a page on which scan and OCR disagree about the number of cards is not joined (" + (SP("63") ? pct(SP("63").joined, SP("63").cards, 0) : "about 94%") + " of Jewar's cards are). Its deleted count is a lower bound until it is re-read.")]));
add(bullet([b("The detector's calibration set. "), run("The two stamp thresholds were set on 35 hand-checked cards of one constituency, and on the first measure clean text-heavy cards and stamped cards overlap (Appendix H); the second measure separates them on those 35 cards. No independent labelled set exists yet for the detector, so its false-negative rate on heavily printed cards is unknown, and a reviewer is right to ask for one; a few hundred cards drawn at random across the three rolls would do.")]));
add(bullet([b("Post hoc analysis. "), run("The audit indicators were fixed before the district was read, but the finding of Section 8.4 was not anticipated: the stamped cards were discovered by following an implausible result, and the deletion analysis of Section 8.6 was reshaped around them. Section 8.4 should be read as exploratory and Sections 8.1–8.3 as the pre-specified part.")]));
add(bullet([b("The age heaps at ages ending in 1. "), run("Our explanation, round ages advanced by one year to the qualifying date, is one of at least two: a date-of-birth field completed with a default day and month would produce the same step. Only the same parts in an earlier roll can distinguish them, and until then Section 8.5 reports the pattern and not its cause.")]));
add(bullet([b("Scope of the duplicate check. "), run("Duplicates were sought by EPIC number within and between the district's three constituencies. An elector enrolled under two numbers, or in another district, is invisible to it; matching on name and relative would find some of these and also produce false matches, and we chose not to match on names alone.")]));

// =====================================================================
// 10 ETHICS
// =====================================================================
add(H1("10. Ethical considerations"));
add(H2("10.1 Personal data and the DPDP Act"));
add(P_("Electoral rolls contain personal data of every adult. Processing it for research is lawful under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 only for a specified purpose and with minimisation; the public-availability exemption does not apply because the electors did not make the data public themselves. All processing in this study is for the purpose of aggregate audit of the revision process."));
add(H2("10.2 Data minimisation and release"));
add(P_("No row-level data, no names and no EPIC numbers are published with this paper. Every figure and table is an aggregate at booth level or above; the smallest group reported anywhere is a booth of 54 electors. The code and the booth-level aggregates behind the figures are released; the extracted rows are not. The benchmark pages are distributed pixelated. The ground-truth transcriptions of real cards (the 30-card development page and the 300-card test set) are kept in a private folder outside the public tree and are available to reviewers; the public tree ships a synthetic page of the same layout with its truth file, and the default evaluation command scores that page."));
add(H2("10.3 No inference of sensitive attributes"));
add(P_("The transliteration layer would make it technically possible to infer religion or caste from names. We do not do so, the released code contains no such function, and we consider any audit that does so to have left the domain of election integrity for that of profiling."));
add(H2("10.4 Dual use"));
add(P_("The same pipeline that enables an audit enables campaign targeting. The mitigations are that the paper publishes method and aggregates, not data; that the indicators were fixed before the district was read; that the code is released for audit and research use; and that the recommendation of Section 9.5 would, if adopted, make the extraction unnecessary."));
add(H2("10.5 Ethics review"));
add(P_("No institutional ethics committee reviewed this study: the author is an independent researcher with no access to one, and no participant was recruited. We give a structured self-assessment in its place and will submit to a venue's ethics process."));
add(bullet([b("Data and source. "), run("Rolls the Election Commission publishes under a statutory duty [13]; no access control was circumvented.")]));
add(bullet([b("Purpose and lawful basis. "), run("Aggregate audit of a public revision process, a research purpose; processing is limited to it and minimised as the DPDP Act requires [14] (Section 10.1).")]));
add(bullet([b("Storage and access. "), run("Extracted rows are held on an access-controlled server and database; transcriptions of real cards are kept outside the public code tree.")]));
add(bullet([b("Release. "), run("Code, a synthetic benchmark page and booth-level aggregates; no names, EPIC numbers or rows (Section 10.2).")]));
add(bullet([b("Risks. "), run("Re-identification, profiling by name and reuse for targeting; no group below a booth is reported, no sensitive attribute is inferred, no claim is made about a named elector (Sections 10.3, 10.4).")]));
add(H2("10.6 Conflict of interest"));
add(P_("This study was carried out as independent research and received no funding. The author is employed by Algo Analytica, a data-analytics firm, whose server and dashboard infrastructure hosted the extracted rows; the firm had no role in the study's design, analysis or conclusions, and no commercial, customer or proprietary data was used."));

// =====================================================================
// 11 LIMITATIONS
// =====================================================================
add(H1("11. Limitations"));
add(bullet([b("No human-read test set. "), run("The 300-card test set was transcribed and adjudicated by a vision-language model; a person has read only the 30-card development page.")]));
add(bullet([b("Known faults not yet fixed. "), run("The dropped first letter of the EPIC series and the Latin B read as 8 in house numbers are measured and reproduced but not fixed, because the fix requires a re-read; every figure describes the rolls as read. A relation printed as अन्य is not parsed, and the reason letter on a deleted card is read but not tabulated.")]));
add(bullet([b("One run without the detector. "), run("Jewar's deleted cards come from the pixel scan joined by page position and are a lower bound.")]));
add(bullet([b("One baseline. "), run("The comparison with a modern recogniser is with Surya only, scored by string containment.")]));
add(bullet([b("No draft roll. "), run("Pre-draft deletions, the quantity the revision debate turned on, were not measured; the comparisons with press figures are consistency checks, not an audit.")]));
add(bullet([b("Detector calibration. "), run("The stamp detector was calibrated on 35 hand-checked cards of one constituency and has no independent labelled test set; its agreement with the independent scan is to within 3%.")]));
add(bullet([b("One district, no committee review. "), run("Gautam Buddh Nagar is among the most urban districts of Uttar Pradesh and its rates say nothing about the state's others; the ethics assessment of Section 10.5 is the author's own, not a committee's.")]));

// =====================================================================
// 12 CONCLUSION
// =====================================================================
add(H1("12. Conclusion and future work"));
add(P_("India's electoral rolls are published in a form that is open in law and closed in practice. A pipeline running on a rented CPU server, with no paid model, read a complete district, " + n(D.rows) + " cards on " + n(C.pages) + " scanned pages, to within 1% of the printed serial numbers, and reproduced to within a few thousand the net change in electors that the Commission's published figures imply. On RQ1 the answer is yes for a booth-level audit and not yet for identity matching in Noida: four test cards in five are read without any error and every age, but one Noida EPIC in eight has the wrong series prefix, a fault the test set found and the synthetic page reproduces. On RQ2, field-specific passes and name consensus, rather than voting, remove most single-pass error, and voting earns its place in the flags. On RQ3 the final roll says that " + pct(D.added, D.rows) + " of the district's cards were added after the draft, young and spread across nearly every section; that " + n(D.deleted ?? 0) + " were printed as deleted; that EPIC numbers appearing twice, within a constituency or across the three, are moves and not duplicates; and that the pre-draft deletions can be measured only from the roll the revision replaced. Future work is that roll; the fix and re-read for the EPIC and house-number faults; a human transcription of the test pages; and templates for other states."));

